(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-anatomy-master-exam-form-e"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form E","category":"Anatomy","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"What are mammary glands related to, and what are ceruminous glands?","choices":["Mammary glands are related to apocrine glands; ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands in the external ear canal containing pigmented granules and lipid droplets","Neither gland type is related to the integumentary system, belonging instead to the endocrine system","Mammary and ceruminous glands are actually the same structure, differing only in their anatomical location and terminology","Mammary glands are related to merocrine glands; ceruminous glands are found in the eyes, not the ear canal as commonly assumed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Mammary glands are related to apocrine glands, and ceruminous glands are modified sweat glands located in the external ear canals, containing pigmented granules and lipid droplets. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the difference between the nail body/plate and the nail bed?","choices":["Both terms describe the identical structure, differing only in the clinical terminology used","Nail body/plate is the external component, bounded by nail grooves and folds; nail bed is the internal component","Nail body/plate is the internal component; nail bed is the external, visible component","Neither structure is part of the nail anatomy, belonging instead to the surrounding cuticle"],"answer":1,"exp":"The nail body/plate is the external component, bounded by nail grooves and folds, while the nail bed is the internal component. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What are the eponychium and hyponychium?","choices":["Eponychium is the lateral nail fold along the sides; hyponychium is the pale lunula near the nail root","Eponychium extends over the nail body at the nail root; hyponychium is the free end of the nail bed","Both terms describe the nail root itself, differing only in embryonic origin","Neither structure is associated with the nail, belonging instead to the surrounding dermis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The eponychium extends over the nail body at the nail root, while the hyponychium is the free end of the nail bed. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Where does the nail root lie, anatomically?","choices":["Within the subcutaneous fat layer only, well below the level of the periosteum","Within the dermis's reticular layer only, anchored by collagen fibers alone","Entirely within the hypodermis, with no connection to bone","Close to the periosteum of the underlying bone, as an epithelial fold"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nail root, an epithelial fold, lies close to the periosteum of the underlying bone. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Why do lacerations or incisions that parallel tension (cleavage) lines usually heal well?","choices":["Because tension lines are located only in the epidermis","Because tension lines contain no collagen fibers at all","Because there is minimal disruption of the collagen fibers","Because parallel incisions always avoid nerve damage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Lacerations or surgical incisions that parallel the tension lines usually heal well because there is minimal disruption of the collagen fibers. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What happens when a laceration or incision is made across (perpendicular to) tension lines?","choices":["The collagen fibers are undisturbed, remaining in their original parallel orientation","The wound heals faster with minimal scarring, since tension is evenly distributed","Greater disruption to collagen fibers, causing the wound to gape and heal with excessive scarring","No difference compared to a parallel incision, since collagen orientation doesn't affect healing"],"answer":2,"exp":"When a laceration or incision is made across (or perpendicular to) the tension lines, there is greater disruption to the collagen fibers, causing the wound to gape and heal with excessive scarring. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What causes stretch marks, and what color changes do they undergo?","choices":["Caused by excessive sun exposure damaging the epidermis; the resulting bands turn from white to red only, then gradually fade","Damage to the epidermis only, with no dermal involvement at all; the resulting bands remain a constant, unchanging color","Caused by bacterial infection of the dermal layer; the resulting bands turn from purple to white only, over several weeks","Damage to collagen fibers in the dermis from rapid/excessive enlargement (e.g., pregnancy); bands of skin appear initially red, become purple, then finally white"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stretch marks result from damage to the collagen fibers in the dermis when an area (like the abdomen during pregnancy) enlarges too large or too fast; the resulting bands of thin, wrinkled skin initially appear red, become purple later, and then finally turn white. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Do stretch marks disappear completely after pregnancy and weight loss?","choices":["They only appear after pregnancy, never fading","They never fade at all, remaining exactly as they first appeared","They disappear completely and immediately","Generally they fade but don't disappear completely"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stretch marks generally fade, but don't disappear completely, after pregnancy and weight loss. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What is a superficial burn, per the classification given?","choices":["Damage extending into the dermis, sparing sweat glands and hair follicles","Damage limited to the subcutaneous fat layer only","Damage to the entire epidermis and dermis, possibly including muscle","Damage to the superficial part of the epidermis only"],"answer":3,"exp":"A superficial burn involves damage to the superficial part of the epidermis only. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is a partial thickness burn, and are sweat glands/hair follicles damaged?","choices":["Damage affects the entire epidermis, dermis, and subcutaneous fat; sweat glands and hair follicles are destroyed","Damage affects the epidermis into the superficial part of the dermis; sweat glands and hair follicles are not damaged","Damage is confined to the epidermis only, with no dermal involvement; sweat glands and hair follicles are destroyed","Damage extends through the epidermis into the deep reticular dermis; sweat glands and hair follicles are destroyed"],"answer":1,"exp":"A partial thickness burn affects the epidermis into the superficial part of the dermis, but sweat glands and hair follicles are not damaged. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is a full thickness burn?","choices":["Damage that never affects sweat glands or hair follicles","Damage limited to the superficial epidermis only","The entire epidermis and dermis (possibly even muscle) are affected","Damage affecting only the superficial dermis, sparing the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"A full thickness burn affects the entire epidermis and dermis, and may even extend to affect muscle. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What structures does the deep layer of the dermis contain?","choices":["Only lymphatic capillaries, with no glandular or hair structures","The stratum corneum, forming the deepest layer of dead, keratinized cells","Hair follicles with their associated arrector pili muscles and sebaceous glands","Only melanocytes, scattered evenly throughout the deep reticular fibers"],"answer":2,"exp":"The deep layer of the dermis contains hair follicles, with their associated smooth arrector muscles (arrector pili) and sebaceous glands. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What ultimately stimulates the Organ of Corti, causing hearing?","choices":["Sound waves cause distortion of the tectorial membrane, stimulating the Organ of Corti","Changes in otolith position, transmitted through the vestibule to the hair cells","Direct contact of sound waves with the modiolus, bypassing the tectorial membrane entirely","Rotation of the head, unrelated to sound, sensed by the semicircular canal ducts"],"answer":0,"exp":"Sound waves ultimately cause a distortion of the tectorial membrane, thus stimulating the Organ of Corti. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the initial sequence of events in the auditory pathway, from sound waves entering the ear to perilymph movement?","choices":["Sound waves directly stimulate the Organ of Corti without any intermediate structures, bypassing the tympanic membrane","Perilymph moves first, causing the tympanic membrane to vibrate afterward, which then sets the ossicular chain in motion","Sound waves enter the external acoustic meatus → tympanic membrane vibrates → ossicles vibrate → stapes vibrates against the oval window → perilymph begins to move","The auditory tube directly transmits sound waves to the cochlea, bypassing the tympanic membrane and ossicular chain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sound waves enter the external acoustic meatus, the tympanic membrane vibrates, this causes vibration of the ossicles, the stapes vibrates against the oval window of the scala vestibuli, and perilymph begins to move. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"After perilymph begins to move, what happens to the scala media, the hair cells of the Organ of Corti, and where is the signal ultimately sent?","choices":["Nerve signals are sent to the brain via CN VII, after pressure on the scala vestibuli directly bends the hair cells","Pressure is put on the scala vestibuli only, with no effect on hair cells, since the scala media stays isolated from perilymph","Pressure is put on the scala media, distorting the hair cells of the Organ of Corti, depolarizing neurons, and sending nerve signals to the brain via CN VIII","The endolymph in the semicircular canals is affected instead, transmitting pressure changes via CN VIII to the vestibular nucleus"],"answer":2,"exp":"As the perilymph moves, pressure is put on the scala media, this pressure distorts the hair cells of the Organ of Corti, this distortion depolarizes the neurons, and nerve signals are sent to the brain via CN VIII (the vestibulocochlear nerve). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"Within the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII) pathway, where do cochlear nerve fibers first synapse after leaving the cochlea?","choices":["The vestibular nucleus","The cochlear nuclei","The auditory cortex directly","The superior olivary nucleus directly, bypassing the cochlear nuclei"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cochlear branch fibers of CN VIII first synapse in the cochlear nuclei, before continuing on to structures like the superior olivary nucleus, inferior colliculus, and medial geniculate nucleus of the thalamus, ultimately reaching the auditory cortex. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Where does the auditory pathway ultimately terminate, and in which lobe is this cortex located?","choices":["The visual cortex, located in the occipital lobe","The primary somatosensory cortex, located in the parietal lobe","The auditory cortex, located in the temporal lobe","The primary motor cortex, located in the frontal lobe"],"answer":2,"exp":"The auditory pathway ultimately terminates at the auditory cortex, located in the temporal lobe. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"The chorda tympani nerve, found within the middle ear cavity, is a branch of which cranial nerve?","choices":["CN V (trigeminal nerve)","CN VIII (vestibulocochlear nerve)","CN VII (facial nerve)","CN IX (glossopharyngeal nerve)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The chorda tympani nerve, seen within the structures of the middle ear cavity, is a branch of CN VII (the facial nerve). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Where do vestibular branch fibers of CN VIII first synapse, and what two general destinations does the pathway continue to?","choices":["Synapse directly in the cerebral cortex, bypassing all brainstem nuclei, before projecting onward to the cerebellum","Synapse at the red nucleus only, with no further connections, terminating before reaching higher processing centers","Synapse at the vestibular nucleus, continuing via vestibulospinal tracts (to the spinal cord) and to the cerebellum, as well as relay to the cerebral cortex via the superior colliculus","Synapse at the cochlear nuclei, identical to the cochlear branch pathway, before diverging toward the auditory cortex"],"answer":2,"exp":"Vestibular branch fibers of CN VIII synapse at the vestibular nucleus, with the pathway continuing via vestibulospinal tracts (to the spinal cord) and to the cerebellum, as well as relaying to the cerebral cortex via the superior colliculus; the vestibular nucleus also connects with CN III, IV, VI, and XI nuclei. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve nuclei are shown connecting with the vestibular nucleus, relevant to coordinating eye and head movement with balance input?","choices":["CN VIII only, with no other cranial nerve connections","CN I, II, V, and VII only","CN IX, X, and XII only","CN III, IV, VI, and XI"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vestibular nucleus is shown connecting with the nuclei of CN III, IV, VI, and XI, relevant to coordinating eye movements (via the oculomotor, trochlear, and abducens nerves) and head/neck movements (via the accessory nerve) with vestibular balance input. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What does the vestibular membrane separate within the cochlear duct system?","choices":["Separates the organ of Corti from the tectorial membrane, preventing contact between hair cell stereocilia and the membrane","Is unrelated to any duct separation within the cochlea, functioning instead solely as support for the modiolus","Separates the tympanic duct from the vestibular duct directly, without the scala media positioned between them","Separates the vestibular duct (scala vestibuli) from the cochlear duct (scala media)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vestibular membrane separates the vestibular duct (scala vestibuli) from the cochlear duct (scala media) within the cochlear spiral. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What are the inner hair cell and outer hair cell, within the Organ of Corti?","choices":["Neither cell type is related to hearing; both instead function as supporting cells anchoring the basilar membrane","Only one type of hair cell exists in the Organ of Corti, arranged in a single row along its entire length","Both are types of hair cells within the Organ of Corti, in contact with the tectorial membrane above the basilar membrane","The inner hair cell is found in the vestibule; the outer hair cell is found in the cochlea's scala vestibuli"],"answer":2,"exp":"The inner hair cell and outer hair cell are both types of hair cells within the Organ of Corti, positioned on the basilar membrane and in contact with the overlying tectorial membrane, contributing to sound transduction. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What type of cartilage is found within the auricle of the external ear?","choices":["Fibrocartilage","Elastic cartilage","No cartilage; the auricle is composed entirely of bone","Hyaline cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"The auricle of the external ear contains elastic cartilage. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the relationship between the oval window and round window, in terms of the structures that interact with each?","choices":["The round window is where the stapes attaches, not the oval window, which instead serves only as a passive pressure-release membrane at the end of the tympanic duct","The stapes base sits at the oval window (transmitting vibration into the scala vestibuli); the round window is a separate membrane-covered opening allowing pressure release at the end of the tympanic duct","Both windows are attached directly to the stapes, transmitting vibration equally into the scala vestibuli and scala tympani","Neither window is related to the auditory ossicles or cochlear ducts, functioning only as passive openings in the temporal bone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The base of the stapes sits at the oval window, transmitting vibrations into the scala vestibuli (vestibular duct), while the round window is a separate, membrane-covered opening that allows pressure release at the end of the tympanic duct pathway. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What does the brachiocephalic trunk give rise to?","choices":["The right common carotid artery and the right subclavian artery","The celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery, supplying the upper abdominal organs","The left subclavian artery only, supplying the left upper extremity","The left common carotid artery only, supplying the left side of the head"],"answer":0,"exp":"The brachiocephalic trunk gives rise to the right common carotid artery (supplying the right side of the head/brain) and the right subclavian artery (supplying the right arm). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"What does the left common carotid artery supply, and what does it divide into?","choices":["Supplies the left leg; divides into the femoral and popliteal arteries below the inguinal ligament","Supplies the pelvic viscera; divides into the internal and external iliac arteries","Supplies the right arm; divides into the radial and ulnar arteries at the elbow","Supplies the left side of the head and brain; divides into internal and external carotid arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"The left common carotid artery supplies blood to the left side of the head and brain, dividing into the internal and external carotid arteries (the internal carotid has no branches until it's in the cranium, while the external carotid begins branching and feeds the neck and face). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"Where does the descending aorta divide into thoracic and abdominal aorta, and at what level?","choices":["At the level of the renal arteries only","It never divides; \"thoracic\" and \"abdominal\" describe the same continuous vessel with no anatomical distinction","At the level of the aortic valve","As it pierces the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm, at the T12 level"],"answer":3,"exp":"The descending aorta, a continuation of the aortic arch, is divided into the thoracic aorta and abdominal aorta as the artery pierces the aortic hiatus of the diaphragm at the T12 level. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What vessels does the thoracic aorta branch to form?","choices":["Renal, suprarenal, and gonadal arteries, supplying the kidneys, adrenal glands, and reproductive organs","Bronchial, pericardial, mediastinal, esophageal, intercostal, and superior phrenic arteries","Common iliac and internal iliac arteries, supplying the pelvis and lower extremities","Renal artery, superior mesenteric artery, and inferior phrenic artery"],"answer":1,"exp":"The thoracic aorta branches to form the bronchial, pericardial, mediastinal, esophageal, intercostal, and superior phrenic arteries. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What major vessels does the abdominal aorta branch to form?","choices":["Only the coronary arteries, which branch from the abdominal aorta near the diaphragm to supply the myocardium","Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery, inferior phrenic/suprarenal/renal/gonadal/lumbar arteries, and common iliac arteries","Only the carotid and subclavian arteries, arising from the abdominal aorta to supply the head and upper limbs","Bronchial, pericardial, and mediastinal arteries, supplying the lungs, heart covering, and chest structures"],"answer":1,"exp":"The abdominal aorta branches to form the celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, inferior mesenteric artery, inferior phrenic arteries, suprarenal arteries, renal arteries, gonadal arteries, lumbar arteries, and the right and left common iliac arteries. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"What organs does the celiac trunk supply?","choices":["Kidneys and adrenal glands only, via the renal and suprarenal branches","Liver, stomach, esophagus, gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas, and spleen","The lower limbs only, via the femoral and popliteal arteries","The heart and lungs, via the coronary and pulmonary circuits"],"answer":1,"exp":"The celiac trunk supplies the liver, stomach, esophagus, gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas, and spleen. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 88"},{"q":"What does the splenic artery (a celiac trunk branch) supply, and what does the left gastric artery supply?","choices":["Both arteries supply the pancreas exclusively, via small branching pancreatic vessels","Splenic artery supplies the pancreas tail; left gastric artery supplies the esophagus","Splenic artery supplies the spleen; left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach","Splenic artery supplies the kidney; left gastric artery supplies the duodenum"],"answer":2,"exp":"The splenic artery, a celiac trunk branch, supplies the spleen; the left gastric artery supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"What does the common hepatic artery branch to form, and what does the cystic artery supply?","choices":["Branches to form the superior mesenteric artery; the cystic artery supplies the spleen's lower pole","Branches to form the hepatic artery proper, right gastric artery, and gastroduodenal artery; the cystic artery supplies the gallbladder","Branches to form only the left gastric artery; the cystic artery supplies the duodenum","Has no further branches; the cystic artery supplies the stomach's lesser curvature"],"answer":1,"exp":"The common hepatic artery branches to form the hepatic artery proper (supplies the liver), the right gastric artery (supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach), and the gastroduodenal artery (supplies the duodenum and inferior greater curvature of the stomach); the cystic artery, a further branch, supplies the gallbladder. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"What does the superior mesenteric artery supply, versus the inferior mesenteric artery?","choices":["Superior mesenteric supplies only the rectum via its terminal branches, while the inferior mesenteric supplies only the stomach via ascending branches","Neither artery supplies any abdominal organ directly, since both function solely as anastomotic connections linking the celiac trunk to the iliac system","Both arteries supply identical territory, running in parallel along the mesentery to double the blood supply reaching the small and large intestine equally","Superior mesenteric: pancreas/duodenum/small intestine/large intestine (via right colic, middle colic, ileocolic arteries); Inferior mesenteric: terminal large intestine/rectum (via left colic, sigmoid, superior rectal arteries)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The superior mesenteric artery supplies the pancreas, duodenum, small intestine, and part of the large intestine (via the right colic, middle colic, and ileocolic arteries), while the inferior mesenteric artery supplies the terminal portion of the large intestine and rectum (via the left colic, sigmoid, and superior rectal arteries). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"What do the internal iliac artery and external iliac artery each supply?","choices":["Both arteries supply identical territory, splitting equally between the pelvic organs and lower extremities","Neither artery is a branch of the common iliac artery, arising instead independently from the descending aorta","Internal iliac: urinary bladder, pelvic walls, external genitalia, medial thigh; External iliac: continues to become the femoral artery, supplying the lower extremities","Internal iliac supplies the lower extremities directly; external iliac remains confined to the pelvis, supplying the bladder, genitalia, and pelvic floor"],"answer":2,"exp":"The internal iliac artery supplies the urinary bladder, walls of the pelvis, external genitalia, and medial side of the thigh, remaining in the pelvis; the external iliac artery continues and becomes the femoral artery, supplying the lower extremities. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What are the five paired (left and right) arteries that branch off the descending aorta?","choices":["Celiac trunk, superior mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric arteries (unpaired)","Coronary arteries and pulmonary arteries","Inferior phrenic, suprarenal, renal, gonadal, and lumbar arteries","Common carotid and subclavian arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"Five paired (left and right) arteries branch off the descending aorta: the inferior phrenic arteries, suprarenal arteries, renal arteries, gonadal arteries, and lumbar arteries. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"Why are the gonadal arteries sometimes called testicular or ovarian arteries instead?","choices":["The terms testicular/ovarian describe a different artery pair entirely, unrelated to the gonadal arteries","The name changes depending on sex once the vessel can be seen supplying the visible reproductive organs","The names are used interchangeably at random, with no anatomical basis","Only male anatomy has gonadal arteries; \"ovarian artery\" is an entirely separate, unrelated vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"The gonadal arteries are called testicular arteries or ovarian arteries depending on sex, once the vessel can be seen supplying the visible reproductive organs (they are simply no longer called \"gonadal arteries\" at that point). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What are the listed functions of the lymphatic system?","choices":["Only regulate body temperature through cutaneous sweat gland activity, blood vessel dilation, and shivering thermogenesis mechanisms","Only produce red blood cells within red bone marrow via the ongoing processes of erythropoiesis, iron recycling, and globin synthesis","Only produce digestive enzymes that break down proteins, carbohydrates, and fats within the small intestine's lumen and mucosa","Produce/maintain/distribute lymphocytes, maintain blood volume and interstitial fluid composition, and provide an alternative transport route for hormones/nutrients/waste"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lymphatic system's functions include producing, maintaining, and distributing lymphocytes, maintaining normal blood volume and interstitial fluid composition, and providing an alternative route for the transport of hormones, nutrients, and waste. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 91"},{"q":"How do lymphatic capillaries compare to vascular (blood) capillaries in diameter and wall thickness?","choices":["Lymphatic capillaries are larger in diameter with thinner walls than vascular capillaries","Lymphatic capillaries are smaller in diameter with thicker walls than vascular capillaries","Both types of capillaries are identical in diameter and wall thickness measurements","Lymphatic capillaries have no walls at all, unlike true vascular capillaries"],"answer":0,"exp":"Lymphatic capillaries are larger in diameter and have thinner walls than vascular capillaries; they also have an irregular outline, anchoring filaments connecting to surrounding connective tissue, and greater permeability. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"How do larger lymphatic vessels (ducts) compare to veins in terms of walls, lumen, and pressure?","choices":["Lymphatic vessels lack valves entirely, unlike veins, and have much thicker walls and higher internal pressure","Lymphatic vessels have thinner walls, larger lumens, and lower pressure than veins, but have valves like most veins","Both vessel types are structurally identical, sharing the same wall thickness, lumen size, and pressure","Lymphatic vessels have thicker walls and higher pressure than veins, and they lack any valves at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Larger lymphatic vessels have thinner walls, larger lumens than veins, and don't have easily identifiable tunics; pressure in lymphatic vessels is lower than in veins, but larger lymphatic vessels do have valves just like most veins (preventing lymph backflow). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What two mechanisms help propel lymph toward the venous system?","choices":["Lymph is not actively propelled; it remains stationary until osmotic pressure builds","Only gravity pulling downward, with no muscular or respiratory assistance","Only the heart's direct pumping action upon the lymphatic vessels","Skeletal muscle contraction and decreased thoracic pressure during inhalation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Skeletal muscles contract to help propel lymph, and inhalation decreases thoracic pressure, which helps move lymph toward the venous system (subclavian veins). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"What is the difference between superficial and deep lymphatics?","choices":["Superficial lymphatics collect from visceral organs only; deep lymphatics are found solely within the subcutaneous layer of the skin","Superficial lymphatics are found in the subcutaneous layer and mucous/serous linings; deep lymphatics collect from skeletal muscles and tissues of the neck/limbs/trunk/viscera","Both sets collect from identical locations, namely the skeletal muscles and deep visceral organs alike, with no subcutaneous drainage","Only one single set of lymphatic vessels exists, collecting uniformly from all body tissues without any superficial-deep distinction"],"answer":1,"exp":"Superficial lymphatics are found in the subcutaneous layer, and in the mucous lining of the digestive/respiratory/urinary/reproductive tracts and serous lining of the pleural/pericardial/peritoneal cavities; deep lymphatics collect lymph from skeletal muscles and tissues of the neck, limbs, trunk, and visceral organs. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"What are the five major lymphatic trunks?","choices":["Lumbar, intestinal, bronchomediastinal, subclavian, and jugular trunks","Only two trunks exist: the thoracic duct and right lymphatic duct","Cervical, axillary, and inguinal lymphatic trunks, three in total","Renal, hepatic, and splenic lymphatic trunks, three in total"],"answer":0,"exp":"The five major lymphatic trunks are the lumbar trunks, intestinal trunks, bronchomediastinal trunks, subclavian trunks, and jugular trunks. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What does the thoracic duct drain, and into what vein does it drain?","choices":["Drains lymph from both sides of the body inferior to the diaphragm and the left side superior to the diaphragm; drains into the left subclavian vein","Drains only the upper body on both sides above the diaphragm; drains into the internal jugular vein directly","Drains the entire body equally from both sides above and below the diaphragm; it drains into the aorta","Drains only the right side of the body above and below the diaphragm; drains into the right subclavian vein"],"answer":0,"exp":"The thoracic duct, the larger lymph duct, drains lymph from both sides of the body inferior to the diaphragm (abdomen, both legs) and the left side of the body superior to the diaphragm (left arm, torso, neck, head); it drains into the left subclavian vein. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 99"},{"q":"What saclike structure does the thoracic duct begin as, and what does it collect lymph from?","choices":["The cisterna chyli; collects only from the right jugular trunk and right subclavian trunk, on the opposite side","A structure called the lymphatic bulb, collecting lymph only from the spleen and the liver directly via a short connecting duct","The cisterna chyli; collects from left/right lumbar trunks, intestinal trunks, left bronchomediastinal trunk, left subclavian trunk, and left jugular trunk","No saclike structure is involved; the duct begins as a simple, uniform tube along its entire length from the pelvis to the neck"],"answer":2,"exp":"The thoracic duct begins as a saclike structure called the cisterna chyli, which collects lymph from the left and right lumbar trunks, intestinal trunks, the left bronchomediastinal trunk, the left subclavian trunk, and the left jugular trunk. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"What does the right lymphatic duct drain, and into what vein does it drain?","choices":["Drains the entire body via all five major lymphatic trunks; it drains directly into the left subclavian vein near the base of the neck","Drains the lower extremities and pelvis via the lumbar trunks; it drains into the inferior vena cava directly near the kidneys and liver","Drains the right arm, right side of the torso/neck/head (via right bronchomediastinal, right subclavian, and right jugular trunks); drains into the right subclavian vein","Drains only the left arm via the left subclavian trunk; it drains into the right subclavian vein instead of the expected left vein"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right lymphatic duct drains lymph from the right arm, right side of the torso, right side of the neck, and right side of the head (specifically via the right bronchomediastinal trunk, right subclavian trunk, and right jugular trunk); it drains into the right subclavian vein. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What do T cells (thymus-dependent cells) stand for, and where do they originate versus become activated?","choices":["Originate in the bone marrow but travel to the thymus gland to become activated (immunocompetent) by thymosin","T cells do not require any activation process, remaining fully functional from the moment of production","Originate in the spleen, becoming activated in the bone marrow through thymosin exposure","Originate and become fully activated entirely within the thymus gland, with no bone marrow origin at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"T cells (thymus-dependent cells) originate in the bone marrow but travel to the thymus gland, where they become activated (immunocompetent) by thymosin; mature T cells then leave the thymus and migrate to red bone marrow, spleen, and other lymphatic tissues. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"What are the four different types of T cells described, and their functions?","choices":["Cytotoxic (attack foreign cells/viruses), helper (stimulate T and B cells), regulatory (moderate immune response), and memory (activate upon re-exposure to an antigen)","All four T cell types perform an identical function, each directly attacking foreign cells and invading viruses without specialization","Only cytotoxic and helper T cells exist, with no regulatory or memory subtypes ever described at all within this course material","B cells, plasma cells, cytotoxic cells, and NK cells, listed together as the four distinct T cell types in this material"],"answer":0,"exp":"The four types of T cells are cytotoxic T cells (attack foreign cells and viruses), helper T cells (stimulate both T and B cells, enhance antibody production), regulatory T cells (moderate immune response), and memory T cells (become activated if the same antigen appears later). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Where do B cells originate and become immunocompetent, and what can they differentiate into?","choices":["Differentiate only into cytotoxic T cells, originating and maturing entirely within the thymus gland","Originate in the thymus, becoming immunocompetent there before differentiating into NK cells alone","B cells cannot differentiate into any other cell type, remaining fixed permanently after bone marrow release","Originate and become immunocompetent in the bone marrow; differentiate into plasma cells (produce antibodies) and memory B cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"B cells originate and become immunocompetent in the bone marrow; mature B cells enter the bloodstream and migrate to peripheral tissues, where they can differentiate into plasma cells (producing antibodies/immunoglobulins that react with antigens) and memory B cells (activated upon re-exposure to the same antigen). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"What do external intercostals do, versus internal intercostals?","choices":["External intercostals elevate the ribs to aid inhalation; internal intercostals depress the ribs to aid exhalation","Neither muscle group affects rib position; both instead act solely on lung tissue elasticity","Both muscle groups perform an identical function, elevating the ribs during inhalation only","External intercostals depress the ribs for exhalation; internal intercostals elevate the ribs for inhalation"],"answer":0,"exp":"External intercostals elevate the ribs to aid in inhalation, while internal intercostals depress the ribs to aid in exhalation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"What structures does the diaphragm attach to, and what is its overall shape/position?","choices":["A large, dome-shaped skeletal muscle attaching to the lower ribs, sternum (xiphoid process), and lumbar vertebrae; separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity","A thin, cartilaginous structure attaching only to the sternum and ribs; it separates the pleural cavity from the pericardial cavity","A flat, sheet-like smooth muscle attaching only to the ribs and clavicle; it separates the two pleural cavities from each other","Attaches only to the vertebral column, with no rib or sternal attachment; it separates the abdominal cavity into left and right halves"],"answer":0,"exp":"The diaphragm is a large, dome-shaped skeletal muscle that separates the thoracic cavity from the abdominal cavity, serving as the floor of the thoracic cavity and roof of the abdominal cavity; it attaches to the lower ribs, sternum (xiphoid process), and lumbar vertebrae, and is the primary muscle of respiration. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What are the three natural openings (hiatuses) of the diaphragm, and at what vertebral levels?","choices":["Only two hiatuses exist: esophagus and aorta","Inferior vena cava (T8), esophagus (T10), and aorta (T12)","Inferior vena cava (T12), esophagus (T8), and aorta (T10)","The diaphragm has no natural openings; all structures pass around it"],"answer":1,"exp":"The diaphragm has three natural openings (hiatuses): the inferior vena cava at T8, the esophagus at T10, and the aorta at T12. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"Besides its respiratory role, what other bodily processes does the diaphragm assist with by increasing/decreasing thoracic and abdominal pressure?","choices":["Sneezing, vomiting, defecating, and childbirth","The diaphragm has no function besides breathing","Only digestion of food, unrelated to pressure changes","Only urination, with no other described processes"],"answer":0,"exp":"The diaphragm helps with increasing or decreasing thoracic and abdominal pressure as needed for processes like sneezing, vomiting, defecating, and childbirth. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"What are examples of accessory respiratory muscles for inhalation?","choices":["The diaphragm and external intercostals (the primary muscles, not accessory)","Scalene muscles, pectoralis minor, sternocleidomastoid, and serratus anterior","Internal intercostals, transversus thoracis, and rectus abdominis muscles only","Internal oblique, external oblique, and rectus abdominis muscles together"],"answer":1,"exp":"Accessory respiratory muscles for inhalation include the scalene muscles, pectoralis minor, sternocleidomastoid, and serratus anterior, assisting the primary muscles (diaphragm, external intercostals) as needed. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What are examples of accessory respiratory muscles for exhalation?","choices":["Diaphragm and external intercostal muscles, which contract together to further expand the thoracic cavity during forced inhalation","Pectoralis minor, serratus anterior, and sternocleidomastoid muscles, which together elevate the rib cage","Internal oblique muscle, external oblique muscle, rectus abdominis, internal intercostal muscles, and transversus thoracis muscle","Scalene muscles and sternocleidomastoid muscles, elevating the first two ribs during forced inhalation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Accessory respiratory muscles for exhalation include the internal oblique muscle, external oblique muscle, rectus abdominis, internal intercostal muscles, and transversus thoracis muscle. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"What are the three types of reflexes involved in respiration?","choices":["Stretch reflexes and tendon reflexes only","Only mechanoreceptor reflexes","Mechanoreceptor reflexes, chemoreceptor reflexes, and protective reflexes","Only chemoreceptor and protective reflexes, with no mechanoreceptor component"],"answer":2,"exp":"There are three different reflexes involved in respiration: mechanoreceptor reflexes, chemoreceptor reflexes, and protective reflexes. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What do mechanoreceptor reflexes respond to, in the context of respiration?","choices":["Changes in lung volume or changes in blood pressure","Changes in body temperature only","Changes in blood glucose levels","Changes in partial pressures of carbon dioxide and oxygen only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Mechanoreceptor reflexes respond to changes in lung volume or changes in blood pressure. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What do chemoreceptor reflexes respond to, and what do they primarily respond to changes in?","choices":["Respond to changes in lung volume and thoracic wall stretch, primarily responding to changes in arterial blood pressure","Respond to changes in partial pressures of carbon dioxide (carbonic acid) and oxygen, primarily responding to changes in pH","Respond only to mechanical stretch of the lungs during deep, forced inhalation efforts","Respond only to temperature changes in inhaled air as it passes through the nasal passages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Chemoreceptor reflexes respond to changes in the partial pressures of carbon dioxide (carbonic acid) and oxygen, primarily responding to changes in pH. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"What are examples of named bronchopulmonary segments of the superior lobe, per the bronchial tree diagram?","choices":["Only a single unnamed segment per lobe","Medial basal and lateral basal segments (inferior lobe segments only)","Apical, posterior, and anterior segments","Segments are named identically across all lobes with no distinction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bronchopulmonary segments of the superior lobe include named segments such as apical, posterior, and anterior (with variations like apicoposterior on the left side). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What are examples of named bronchopulmonary segments of the inferior lobe, per the bronchial tree diagram?","choices":["Superior, anterior basal, lateral basal, medial basal, and posterior basal segments","The inferior lobe has no distinctly named bronchopulmonary segments whatsoever","Only the apical and posterior segments, which are superior lobe segments","Only the lateral and medial segments, which are middle lobe segments"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bronchopulmonary segments of the inferior lobe include the superior, anterior basal, lateral basal, medial basal, and posterior basal segments. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"What structures create grooves on the medial surfaces of the right and left lungs, respectively?","choices":["Right lung has a groove for the esophagus; left lung has a groove for the aorta","Both lungs have identical grooves for the same structure","Right lung has a groove for the aorta; left lung has a groove for the esophagus","Neither lung has any grooves on its medial surface"],"answer":0,"exp":"The right lung's medial surface has a groove for the esophagus, while the left lung's medial surface has a groove for the aorta (in addition to its cardiac notch). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 43"}]};
